; A KEYED map member whose type is a plain group. The key claims one map entry, and that entry's
; VALUE slot holds exactly ONE item — but a keyless group has no single-item form, so the only
; emission available is splicing its members in flat. The named spelling used to do exactly that at
; exit 0: `t = { c: kv }` wrote `a1 6163 6161 07 6162 08`, five items after a one-entry map header,
; which an interoperating decoder reads as `{'c': 'a'}` plus four trailing bytes — and no decoder
; was generated at all (`map_record_deser_refusals` declined the record, since a map's members may
; arrive in any order and `deserialize_as_embedded_group` reads a fixed sequence). A serialize-only
; crate emitting bytes only itself can interpret is worse than a refusal: exit 0, it compiles, and
; nothing tells the author the wire is wrong.
; Every KEYED spelling reaches the same splice, so the guard is on the member's RESOLVED type
; rather than on any one of them:
;   - `t`  the bare named member.
;   - `u`  a TAG around it — a tag is an encoding operation, so the member type is still the group,
;          and the serializer wrote the tag head and then spliced.
;   - `v`  `?` on it, which aborted on `assertion failed: !config.optional_field` instead.
;   - `al` an ALIAS to the group, caught through shallow alias resolution (it panicked at
;          generation on a struct that was never materialized).
;   - `x`  a single-entry map GROUP-CHOICE arm carrying the member, which reaches the refusal
;          through the enum seam instead of the record one — its serializer and deserializer did
;          not even agree with each other.
;   - `y`  a multi-entry map group-choice arm, which reaches it through the record seam.
; Green neighbours, kept here so a refactor that over-widens the guard shows up as a message flip:
;   - `remedy` (with `rk`/`rk_arr`) is what the message names — a NAMED array rule gives the slot
;     the one nested item it needs and generates a FULL codec, deserialize included.
;   - `arr_rep` (with `ck`) is the ARRAY-representation placement of a plain group, supported
;     because an array's emitted length scales with the group's arity, so splicing there IS
;     conformant.
;   - `q` is a KEYLESS single-entry map group-choice arm (`{ n: uint // kv }`), supported because
;     the referenced struct owns its own keys and writes a conformant 2-entry map.
; The inline array member (`{ c: [kv] }`) is deliberately NOT here and is NOT the remedy: it
; collapses the group to Array rep and is refused by the conflicting-representations guard, whose
; own message owns that shape.
; Each family gets its own group so the reps stay consistent within one file — a group used both as
; a map member and as an array element would earn the conflicting-representations rejection instead
; and mask what these rows are here to hold.
kv = (a: uint, b: uint)
kv_alias = kv
t = { c: kv }
u = { c: #6.1(kv) }
v = { ? c: kv }
al = { c: kv_alias }
x = { n: uint // c: kv }
y = { n: uint // c: kv, d: uint }
q = { n: uint // kv }
rk = (m: uint, n: uint)
rk_arr = [rk]
remedy = { c: rk_arr }
ck = (uint, uint)
arr_rep = [ c: uint, ck ]
